00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.790 align:start position:0%
 
are you hoping to see from conference  well look I think the test that  everyone's been talking about for the  last week is whether or not the  government is basically being fiscally  responsible I think there's another test  at Conservative Party Conference the  government set out in this growth plan  nobody's really paid any attention to it  but a set of reforms to drive growth  that are normally not the kind of  reforms you hear from the conservative  party immigration reform to make sure we  have the skills we need planning reform  very often conservative councils not  supportive of planning reform Net Zero  is back in fashion onshore wind is back  in fashion now that's what businesses  around the country were quite heartened  by but we are nervous as to whether or  not the conservatives really mean what  they say so I'm hoping this week to hear  a little bit more from the chancellor  the Prime Minister and in The Fringe  meetings that really the conservative  party do put two and a half percent  growth of primary importance to those  other conservative predilections  policies preferences that's interesting  actually isn't it because look this is  government it's all about going for  growth and yet the two things that  you've been talking about there  immigration and planning reform as well  I mean they are in particular things  that is quite difficult for  conservatives to push through do you  think there might be a bit of opposition  to that yes I think there might be and  look those are probably the two things  that come up most with businesses around  the country in terms of what do we need  to drive growth everybody is short of  Labor and skills and everybody is trying  to get projects moving in a country  that's hard to get them moving now full  credit to the chancellor and the Prime  Minister for putting that in their plan  uh and I think we'll all be wanting to  see that they really mean what they say  what what's what is your sense on that  you know because the Home Secretary  Suella Brotherman has talked about  driving immigration controlling  immigration levels keeping them down are  you have you had any sense from the  Prime Minister herself that she is  prepared to look at immigration well not  yet other than the chancellor raised it  and said they're going to be proposals  coming forward look everybody knows and  we saw this at last Conservative Party  Conference last year that we aren't  going to have uncontrolled immigration  and there isn't a business in the the  country that's looking for uncontrolled  immigration but the idea that you can  overnight close down the borders and not  allow in critical skills that we clearly  aren't going to grow in Britain soon  that's just putting growth second not  first and surely now after the events of  the last week the lesson is growth comes  first not second and you see believe  crochet conferenced many other people  would say right now that inflation  should come first they're looking at  their household bills and they're deeply  deeply concerned they're looking at  what's happened to mortgages and they  are frankly terrified about what will  happen if they are up for a renewal  anytime soon  and surely the biggest tax cutting  program for half a century is  Stoke inflation  well I think it it raises two risks  doesn't it the first is inflationary and  the second is and I think this is what  the markets were reacting to that it  undermines your ability to bring down  debt in the long run and I think that's  right so you have to look at whether or  not these tax cuts are going to drive  growth now we don't comment a lot on  personal income tax it's not really our  business I'm not convinced those cuts  are going to drive growth so just to be  Crystal Clear yeah you're talking about  the decision to scrap the additional  rate of tax for everyone earning over  150 000 you don't think that will really  move the Garland if you ask me for 30  measures that are going to drive up  growth I wouldn't put it in the top 30.  now the chancellor may feel differently  he might want to set out why he thinks  it's going to stimulate growth at the  same time clearly reversing employers  National Insurance clearly not putting  up corporation tax by six points those  things will be helpful to growth and  competitiveness so look I think there's  a lot of reasons why the markets uh find  the budget not credible I think you're  alluding to some of them that are right  but I think the chance to the Prime  Minister now need to come forward and  explain explain  what they've done why they've done it  why they think it'll work and at the  very least not row back from some of the  commitments that they have made to  supply side reform  um how about spending cuts  um are you concerned about the idea  about a cutting spending yes but look I  think when you borrow 600 billion pounds  if you take the 450 of the pandemic the  150 of the energy crisis you're trying  to bring down debt in the medium term  you're clearly going to have a plan  that's going to have a mix of spending  cuts perhaps some tax Rises I would have  thought as well as some growth now the  exact balance of that we are awake to  hear what the government's view is but  I'd be surprised if you could keep  growing public spending as fast as you  can but you need to be careful because  we all know that after the pandemic  there are critical public services that  need to be restored that's why the  medium term plan is going to be tough  work tough work  um it's there a fairness argument out  here as well I know you obviously speak  on health of businesses that businesses  employ people don't know as well is  there a fairness argument when you look  at what has been not said or rather not  said about operating benefits and that  their personal tax level Cuts there is  absolutely a fairness argument I I have  spoken to businesses all week and the  first thing on their mind was the  fairness argument I don't accept that  you have to trade off growth with  fairness I think you should have growth  and you should have Fair distribution of  wealth and I think that's the economy we  should be trying to build so yes I'm  absolutely as are most of the businesses  I speak to worried about the cost of  living crisis almost above everything  that's interesting what would you like  to see it happen on that well look I  think let's wait for the chancellor to  come forward I'm not going to try and  pray out what needs to come we've got  ourselves into a difficult position by  the way we were always going to I think  one of the things the public has to  accept is that if we're going to have  450 billion pounds covered bail line the  government have done a 150 billion pound  energy payout there weren't going to be  any easy choices so I cut the government  some slack for for that but I think they  need to restore fiscal credibility  exactly as you say that's to do with  inflation and debt and they need to  follow through this week and some things  that aren't very popular in conservative  circles those are the reforms that will  genuinely get the economy growing do  they also need to bring forward uh  forecasts from the office the budget  responsibility they're not due till the  end of November yes I think that's right  look as soon as they can do them the  better but let's be honest we need to  get them right and getting them right  involves the government's suggesting the  plans they want to do the obr giving  them fiscal implications of those plans  it's an iterative process so I don't  want to rush it so that it's done badly  it needs to be done well but the sooner  we see it I think the more we'll all  know where we're headed Let's cross  saying this morning that she won't be  publishing them any earlier I think  that's the right decision look I think  if it takes that long to get a good plan  take that long what you'll need to do  instead in the interim period is  demonstrate on a daily weekly basis that  you are absolutely committed to growth  that you're concerned about inflation  and debt and whatever the government can  do to give the market that reassurance  given that the markets are lending us  the money I think they'll find a way to  do that

